Title:
BindlinkSentinel

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
BindlinkSentinel is a user-mode Windows detection sensor prototype aimed at spotting bind-link abuse via the Bind Filter minifilter (`bindflt.sys`), an EDR-evasion technique described by Bitdefender (2026). It focuses on detecting when “trusted” paths are transparently redirected to attacker-controlled content without on-disk artifacts, undermining path-based security assumptions. The tool monitors a configurable watchlist of high-value binaries/DLLs and uses Authenticode verification (`WinVerifyTrust`), SHA-256 hash baselining, and decoy (bait) directory monitoring to raise alerts. It also checks `bindflt` service state and provides an integration point for enumerating live bind mappings (left intentionally unimplemented due to version-specific, undocumented message formats). Implementations are provided in C++ (Win32) and C# (.NET Framework 4.8), plus a C# simulation harness that emulates the observable effects of shadow redirection without creating real bind links. It’s primarily useful for Blue Teams/defenders and detection engineers validating coverage against bind-link-based defense impairment and execution-flow hijacking.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Bind Filter minifilter (`bindflt.sys`) and bind-link filesystem virtualization
- Authenticode signature validation via `WinVerifyTrust`
- File integrity baselining with SHA-256 (BCrypt / .NET `SHA256`)
- User-mode filesystem change monitoring (`ReadDirectoryChangesW`, `FileSystemWatcher`)
- Service state checks (`ServiceController`) and bindflt presence validation
- Minifilter user-mode comms / mapping enumeration via `fltlib` (integration point)

Use Cases:
- Detect shadow redirection of trusted binaries/DLLs used for AMSI/EDR bypass
- Monitor high-value system paths for signature breakage and hash divergence
- Deploy decoy directories/files to catch staging/recon activity around bind-link setups
- Lab validation of bind-link abuse detections using Bitdefender `bindutil` toolset
- Build a starting point for implementing bindflt mapping enumeration in a defender agent

Keywords:
bindflt.sys, Bind Filter, bind links, filesystem virtualization, EDR evasion, AMSI bypass, AppLocker bypass, WinVerifyTrust, Authenticode, SHA-256, BCrypt, ReadDirectoryChangesW, FileSystemWatcher, fltlib, minifilter communication port, path redirection, silo-binding, process-binding, file-binding, Windows internals